<section id="leaveners" class="leavener-comparison" aria-labelledby="leavener-heading">
  <h2 id="leavener-heading">Two white powders. Different jobs.</h2>
  <div class="leavener-grid">
    <article>
      <p class="eyebrow">Baking soda</p>
      <h3>The base</h3>
      <dl>
        <dt>In the container</dt><dd>Sodium bicarbonate.</dd>
        <dt>In a quick-bread recipe</dt><dd>
          Needs an appropriate acidic ingredient, such as cultured buttermilk.
        </dd>
        <dt>After mixing</dt><dd>
          The acid reaction starts when ingredients meet in the wet batter.
        </dd>
      </dl>
    </article>
    <article>
      <p class="eyebrow">Double-acting baking powder</p>
      <h3>A balanced mixture</h3>
      <dl>
        <dt>In the container</dt><dd>
          Bicarbonate plus acid salts, usually with starch to keep the mixture dry.
        </dd>
        <dt>In a quick-bread recipe</dt><dd>Brings its own acid for its leavening reaction.</dd>
        <dt>After mixing</dt><dd>Releases some gas when moistened, then more as it heats.</dd>
      </dl>
    </article>
  </div>
  <p class="comparison-note">
    A recipe may use both. Its acidic ingredients, flavor, browning and rise all matter, so swapping
    equal spoonfuls changes the formula.
  </p>
</section>
